{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 11, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3.5-397b-a17b", "content": "Raymond III served as the Count of Tripoli from 1152 until his death in 1187 and he played a pivotal role within the Kingdom of Jerusalem during the twelfth century. His life story involves complex political maneuvering alongside significant military engagements that shaped the fate of the Crusader states in the Levant. He often found himself at odds with other nobles while trying to maintain stability against rising external threats from Muslim forces led by Saladin.\n\n*Early Life and Succession*\nRaymond inherited his title after his father Raymond II suffered assassination by the Order of Assassins in 1152. The young count faced immediate challenges because he was only a child when he took power so regents managed affairs until he reached maturity. He grew up within the court culture of the Latin East and learned the intricacies of feudal politics while observing the fragile balance between Christian principalities and their Muslim neighbors. His early years defined his cautious approach to diplomacy and war since he understood the precarious nature of Crusader holdings in Syria and Palestine.\n\n*Regency and Political Struggles*\nThe count acted as regent for Baldwin IV who suffered from leprosy and could not rule without assistance during certain periods. Raymond sought peace treaties with Nur ad-Din and later Saladin because he believed confrontation would lead to disaster for the weakened kingdom. This stance created bitter enemies among the aggressive faction led by Guy of Lusignan and Raynald of Châtillon who favored offensive campaigns against Muslim territories. Internal strife weakened the realm significantly as noble families fought for influence over the sick king and the succession line while Raymond tried to prevent civil war through negotiation and compromise.\n\n*The Battle of Hattin and Legacy*\nDisagreements over strategy culminated i